Hi all,
I have a general question for alphas: from time to time when I try to compile 
a program for my alpha (PC164), I run into problems with doubles. For example

{
	double a;

	if ( a ){
		printf( "love\n" );
	} else {
		printf( "peace\n" );
	}
}

seems to run w/o problems on an i386 and produces "peace" as output. Now, on 
an alpha (can't test it right now, but try it out and tell me), it segfaults, 
cause 'a' is not defined. Seems logical to me. But as it runs on i386, lots 
of programs have this kind of logic (gnome-print had it, breakout2 had it, 
scribus had it, noteedit has it (note the had/has and try to figure out which 
program I'd like to run on my alpha ;-) ).
 Now my question: is it possible to remove this behaviour with a good 
compiler-switch? Because going over these programs, searching for 
non-initialised doubles gets on my nerves. OK, I should, as it is really bad 
programming, and I could contribute like this (and did sometimes). But 
still... Any hints?

Ineiti

PS: if this 'magic' compiler switch doesn't exist, I have a question about gdb 
with libraries and how to use the '-g' switch in this case. Just to find 
those #@$%-variables...




