It has always been my impression that testing a float for 0 is in 
general not a good idea. If you set it to 0 you will usualy get the 
response you expect but there are a number of issues around floating 
point numbers and the formats they are kept in. Like +0 and -0 not being 
quite the same as 0. The other thing is that you can get a number very 
close to 0 but is not quite 0. There is a certain amount of ambiguity 
about floting point numbers and it just does not seem good form to use 
them as boolean values. There are more floating point formats out there 
than you can shake a stick at and you need to be carefull when using them.

