"Darren Dupre" <darrendupre@houston.rr.com> writes:

> I have two 4.3GB SCSI drives that I would like to configure as a
> software RAID-0 device.
> 
> This does not seem possible since SRM requries BSD disklabels and since
> software RAID wont work with BSD disklabels.

It does, just the autodetection won't work. So your root partition
would have to be non-RAID, but all other data could. I'm currently
using that setup.

> Is there any solution? Would using MILO support booting from normal DOS
> partitions (that the RAID driver requires)?

MILO certainly supports DOS partitions, after all, it was designed for
dual boot with Windows NT. I don't think it can load a kernel from a
raid, though, so you'd have to copy it onto the FAT partition.

I'd advise to use SRM though, as it provides better PAL-Codes to
Linux.

-- 
	Falk




