>
>Hi all,
>
>        I have an AlphaPC 164LX but now I got a problem trying to install Red 
>Hat Linux 7.3 (Kernel 2.4.18-3) on my Dell Precision 330 with the 3Dlabs 
>Wildcat II 5110 graphic card.
>
>The installation is a success but Redhat used VESA (generic) driver for the
>X-Server. The actuall VideoRam is 128Mb but RH made it 2Mb.
>Worst of all, my Dell monitor is Dell 2000FP but now RH installation displays
>only a resolution 640x480@60Hz while Windows 2000 got a resolution
>1280x1024@75Hz.
>
>I know that there is a commercial driver for it from XiG. But I didn't expect
>such a bad resolution using a generic driver, before buying the XiG $299 driver.
>
>Can anyone help to recommend a generic driver for a higher resolution
>like 1280x1024 for the time being?

3Dlabs hardware is very very rare to come across.  It is 
generally only used in high end graphics situations, and is quite 
rare compared to ATI, Nvidia, Matrox and other commodity 
hardware.  As such, we rarely if ever receive bug reports against 
this hardware.  I personally have no 3Dlabs hardware, and I know 
only a few people that do have any.  Bryce and Alan have a Oxygen 
VX1.  Alan Hourihane likely has some 3Dlabs hardware as well, as 
I believe he maintains the driver.

If you're having problems with 3Dlabs hardware, I suggest you 
contact Alan Hourihane directly for troubleshooting.  Also, 
please don't refer to it as "Red Hat made it 2Mb", as that is not 
very accurate as to what is going on.  The support for 3Dlabs 
hardware in XFree86 is only as good as the amount of people out 
there who have the hardware and are working on it.  It has 
relatively little at all to do with Red Hat.

If hardware is misdetected, it is generally hardware bugs, or 
video driver bugs, or the hardware is just not supported 
officially at all.




