On Friday 12 July 2002 01:49, Arnim Wiezer wrote:
> Hi !
>
> Yesterday I tried to install RH 7.2 on one of our ES45 boxes. Everything
> seems to run perfect till the first boot from harddisk. It seems that
> the initrd file is not there. When I try to boot without the initrd
> parameter, the booting sequence stops after booting the kernel (before
> the fs gets mounted).

This has been known to happen on a few systems. It tends to occur during 
upgrades.  The problem does not occur with clean installs. This problem does 
ot occur when upgrading from 7.1 -> 7.2, but there is  a complex technical 
reason why that will fails on a 7.0 -> 7.2 upgrade installations.

>
> As I mounted the harddisk with 7.2 in another machine I noticed that the
> initrd file is really not there. Is this a bug ? Does anyone know how to
> circumvent this problem ?

You may be lucky and the initrd won't be needed (think of 
it as a repository for kernel modules that are needed for booting)
so the boot line

         b /boot/vmlinuz-2.4.9-32 root=/dev/sdb1

may simply just work.

Otherwise you'll have to boot from the CD in rescue mode, and then at 
the # prompt
chroot /mnt/sysimage
mount -a
mkinitrd -f -v /boot/initrd-2.4.9-32.img 2.4.9-32
sync
reboot

I hope this helps.

Best Regards,


--George




