Anyone running RHL 7.2/Alpha on a UP2000 with 2 CPU's?

I've been having some problems trying to get SMP to work 
correctly.  Myself and Bryce have experimented with different 
things to try and determine any potential problems.

Both CPU's are identical stepping, etc. 666Mhz 21264B, with 4Mb 
cache.  Running the system UP with either processor and a UP 
kernel works just fine.  Booting UP kernel with both processors 
installed also works fine.

If I boot with both CPU's active with any SMP kernel from 2.4.3 
to the one included with RHL 7.2 (2.4.9-32.5smp), the same 
problem occurs:

When sd_mod is loaded, I get:

kernel BUG at slab.c:865

It does NOT happen every single time however, which is weird.  It 
occurs almost always until I pull out the NIC (3c905b).  
Then after that it occurs only about 30% of the time.

Regardless of wether or not the NIC is present, or the above bug 
occurs, at some point in time, usually right when sd_mod is 
loaded, I also get:

flush_tlb_page:  timeout
smp_imb: timeout

These timeout messages occur a few times.  Sometimes the kernel 
makes it through to init, other times it hangs with the 
slab.c:865 kernel panic above.  When it does make it to userland, 
and initscripts are processed, randomly the flush_tlb etc. is 
printed to the console.  I occasionally can get it to even start 
up X now, and the machine seems to run ok with SMP, however those 
two messages are echoed to the console occasionally, and in 
bursts, and also to /var/log/messages.

I'm booting off a Seagate Barracuda on the non LVD SCSI channel 
the UP2000 has onboard.  The only PCI cards in this machine are a 
Matrox Millenium II, and the 3com 3c905b. I've removed both 
cards, and tried with a Voodoo 3 PCI, and a few other PCI cards 
too, with no observed change.

It appears to me that the SCSI driver module might be the problem
here.  The really odd thing however, is that Bryce has an
identical machine more or less, and it works just fine for him.
Same CPU's, etc.

I was using an older SRM than he was, and have since updated it 
to the latest SRM 5.8-81.  The machine still has the same 
problems however.

My next attempt will be to boot SMP with an IDE drive instead of 
using the SCSI drive to see if that is the problem.

Anyone else experienced this sort of weirdness on UP2000?


