On Mon, 29 Jul 2002, Scott Sewall wrote:

> 
> Anyone have experience with an API Networks CS20?

A little :)
 
> I'm mostly interested in it's ability to load and run Linux and FreeBSD.

Linux will run w/o trouble, the only catch being you need a rev of the 
kernel that supports the onboard SCSI (Ultra3). With anything fairly 
recent you should be fine. There's also a package that will allow you to 
query the temp sensors and fan speed etc....

FreeBSD I'm not sure of. It's basically a DP264 type motherboard with EV68 
CPUs so from that point of view you should be fine. There's no 
keyboard/mouse ports so it's controlled via the serial port (I would 
assume FreeBSD can handle serial installs ?).  The other differences would 
be the ALi southbridge chip and the SCSI.

> I suspect it's a repackaged DEC/Compaq  motherboard, but would like to 
> make sure before I invest any green.

Actually, for once it's the other way around. API designed and built the 
CS20 then sold the design to Q who have since produced the DS20L.

--rdp


-- 
Rich Payne
