I'm pretty sure the 32MB of memory is not sufficient for the install. =
This is based on experience and earlier recommendations from this list.

Unfortunately, I've been having a difficult time finding memory that =
will work.  I've ordered used memory on-line; first batch didn't work, =
replacement batch with parity didn't work.

In a recent search of the web, I found a web site in Dutch that =
indicated "36 bit ( true parity,ecc ) 60/70 ns fp simms" are needed.

Recently Jeff Donsbach at HP posted to this list recommending "72 Pin =
Fast Page Mode ECC SIMMS w/ Parity. "

Look's like I need to find some ECC memory.

-- Scott

Jay Estabrook wrote:


On Mon, Jul 29, 2002 at 08:16:57AM -0700, Scott R. Sewall wrote:

Thank you for your notes.  I have a XL300 that I've been trying to
install Linux.  I'm sure your notes will help.  btw, how much RAM
does your system have?  I have 32MB, which doesn't seem to be
enough.


Whoa! 32MB is going to be barely enough to do an install; once you get
done, if you want to run X, I suggest a minimum 128MB. I suggest poking
around at computer flea-markets and "salvage" shops, you should be able
to get ahold of some 16MB SIMMs (8 slots -> 128MB) or 32MB SIMMS (256MB)
for a reasonable fee, given that all new machines don't use them...

Good luck.

 --Jay++

