> There are two issues here
> 
> 1) RISC processors, in general, require more instructions than a CISC
> processor to do the same amount of work. Anytime you want to update
> something in memory, you first need to load it into a register, modify it,
> then store it back to memory. CISC processor can usually use 1 instruction
> to update the memory location directly. Therefore, text segment sizes of
> programs on CISC would most likely be larger than the same program on a
> CISC machine (Alpha vs x86 for example).
> 
> 2) Alpha Liunx is 64 bit. Any data of type "long" is going to be twice the
> size than on x86. With 64 bit pointers, any pointer data is going to be
> twice the size of an x86. C structs with pointers and longs get big real
> fast.
> 
> So, yes, maxing out the memory in your system certainly can't hurt!
> 
> Jeff D
